Image: evo-flight-lab:sealed, linux/arm64, network disabled.
GCC: Debian 14.2.0-19; Clang: Debian 19.1.7; glibc: 2.41.
Sources: tests/fixtures/<name>.c, unchanged between compilers/optimization levels.
Command for each compiler and optimization level:
  CC -std=gnu11 -OLEVEL -fno-omit-frame-pointer -fno-optimize-sibling-calls SOURCE -o probe
  ./probe

name                            gcc-O0 gcc-O2 clang-O0 clang-O2 (exit status)
variable-array-objects          0      0      0        0
variable-size-type-query        0      0      0        0
assembly-general-update         0      0      0        0
assembly-tied-width             0      0      0        0
null-conditional-qualification   0      0      0        0
mixed-record-varargs            0      0      0        0
relative-label-table            0      0      0        0
atomic-read-modify-write        0      0      0        0
integer-complex-values          0      0      0        0
ancestor-return-address         1      1      0        0

Both compilers warn on the immediate const discard in
null-conditional-qualification.c. GCC: -Wdiscarded-qualifiers;
Clang: -Wincompatible-pointer-types-discards-qualifiers. Neither rejects it.
No warning was removed or promoted to a passing execution result.

Ancestor return addresses are NOT consensus: GCC returns zero for level 1,
Clang reads the saved ancestor LR. This confirms the existing Appendix D
"Ancestor frame addresses and the return-address disagreement" rule; EvoCC's
E2913 refusal is retained. The positive assertion in the initial RED was
incorrect, not evidence of an implementation defect. Frame traversal is a
separate already-implemented operation.

