The pointer-tie reduction was RED: 1 failed / 0 passed, zero build errors,
E3021 for matching constraint "0" (pointer input, 32-bit output). The shared
asm input path now exposes the pointer as a 64-bit address before the existing
narrowing conversion. The IR validator still requires equal tied types.
language_semantics: 24 passed / 0 failed; inline_assembly_lowering: 46 passed /
0 failed. The expanded tied-pointer IR comparison also passes for object and
function pointers with 16-, 32- and 64-bit outputs; it produces the same IR as
an explicit unsigned-long address cast. A narrower input tied to a wider
output remains refused. These checks postdate the 2432-test gate above.

Direct vector declarations exposed a further real E3101 in original pr123625:
vector-declaration-attributes.c covers direct local/global declarations,
comma-separated names, arrays, pointers, record members, parameters/array
parameter adjustment and a vector result. Initial RED: 1 failed, no build
errors (E2201 on the second comma-separated name). The previously tested
typedef form was green; direct written declarations also failed independent
lowering/interpreter type-identity checks.
GCC/Clang O0/O2 on the final fixture all exit 0. The parser now applies the
attribute to the scalar base through declarator layers, including each fresh
comma-list declarator. Both C-HIR execution and lowering share the check that
the written size, base type and qualifiers justify the canonical vector.
Changing the size/base or removing the applied attribute remains rejected.
Both interpreters and both native assembler/linker combinations exit 0.
Focused checks: language_semantics 25/25, integer_vectors 10/10,
inline_assembly_lowering 46/46, lower library 3/3 and resolved forms 24/24.

